{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788214964,"nonce":"+XO3w/J3ZcMyBCoVwXs45H/Zbx8=","message":"CLOSED — acceptance verified personally, orchestrator, BORGES, 2026-08-31.\nMerged on main at `b3d79a0` plus one orchestrator fix `c73e58e`.\n\nTHE FRAMING QUESTION WAS ANSWERED, NOT INHERITED — and that is the best thing here\n\nThe spec said \"morning checklist\". `checklists/README.md` rejects the premise and\nsays why: the agent has no mornings. Its life is act -\u003e advance -\u003e read, and the\nonly moments in that loop are an act about to be taken, an event that halted the\nadvance, and the read when control returns. A daily sweep pays for what the\ndigest already carries, arrives a day late for act-keyed checks, and is slower\nthan vanilla's own tutor for incident response. So the checklist is three files\nkeyed to real moments — `turn.md` (zero extra queries, thresholds over the digest\nin hand), `triggered.md` (act- and event-keyed), `daily.md` (only what drifts on\na day scale). The daily rung is justified mechanically rather than by habit: its\nvariables move over days, 4.2 already caps early advances near a day so the sweep\npiggybacks a pause the loop was making anyway, and one verdict per item per day\nis exactly 4.4's evidence row.\n\nOne insight I did not have and would not have written: **the checklist is spec\n1.6's `condition` matcher run by hand.** `advance until:` halts only on events;\nnothing fires when a continuous value crosses a line. So every `turn.md`\ntrip-wire is a state predicate the agent evaluates itself, and each item carries\na `becomes:` line naming the predicate it turns into once 1.6 lands. The file\nstates its own exit strategy.\n\nVERIFIED PERSONALLY\n\n- **A new source finding, checked and correct:** `Alert_LowFood` is muzzled for\n  the first 150,000 ticks by `if (TicksGame \u003c 150000f) return false;` — and it\n  uses `TicksGame`, not `GenDate.DaysPassed`, which is why the session-4\n  curriculum audit's sweep for time-scoped alerts missed it. Combined with the\n  1.6-nutrition/day correction from 2d9a1da, the food floor is derived rather\n  than guessed, and the doc says which half is which.\n- Invented thresholds are marked `(proposed)`. Items that would need a trend are\n  in an explicit blocked section naming 2d9a1da, with the reason stated plainly:\n  \"a level dressed up as a slope is a lie with a citation.\"\n- The three warnings from the handoff all held: the growing-zone correction\n  survived, the combat-role formula still says its constants are the\n  orchestrator's and not Evan's, and nothing claims a trend it cannot measure.\n- `FirefoamPopper` is genuinely in `templates/power-room.ir.json`'s grid, not\n  merely named in prose — the escalation ladder's endpoint is real.\n\nTHE ONE FAILURE, AND THE FIX (`c73e58e`)\n\nAcceptance requires the dry-run to yield **a lesson file AND a checklist item AND\na template patch**. Two of three existed. There was no lesson file, and no row in\n`playbook/INDEX.md` — so the document's own claim that a fresh session surfaces\nthe lesson at \"position 1's index line\" was false; that line did not exist.\n\nThe reasoning for omitting it was that this loss is pre-learned, so the lesson\nstarts already graduated into the checklist trigger and the template. That is\ndefensible and is now recorded IN the file. But it needed the artifact anyway,\nfor a reason worth keeping: **a graduated lesson with no index entry is\nindistinguishable from a lesson nobody ever learned.** The ladder is meant to\nmove checks out of prose, not erase the record that the colony paid for them.\nAdded `playbook/zzzt-letter-is-a-fire-already-burning.md` with a `graduated-to:`\nfield, and its INDEX row marked **graduated**. Re-ran the grep the document\ninvites: lesson 1, index row 1, checklist item 1, template patch 1.\n\nThe general convention this implies — every graduated lesson keeps an index row\nnaming where it went — is left to 4.4 (`d32eadd`), which owns retirement\nevidence, rather than invented here as a schema change.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DEMONSTRATE\n\n- **The dry-run was not run against a real journal.** It is worked inline in\n  `postmortem.md` from a pinned synthetic six-line excerpt, and its three outputs\n  converge on artifacts the repo already carried. That convergence is the\n  document's stated demonstration, and it is a weaker form than \"given a journal\n  the playbook has NOT pre-learned, the procedure produces new artifacts\" — which\n  is the claim that actually matters and which only 4.3 can test.\n- No checklist item has been executed against a live bench. Every threshold is\n  reasoned, none is calibrated from a run.\n- PROCESS SLIP, recorded rather than reverted: the worker committed straight to\n  local `main` instead of a `spec/4.1-playbook` branch. Markdown only, nothing\n  else was in flight on this box, and no collision occurred — but the standing\n  rule since session 4 is a worktree and branch per worker.","files":null}]}